   485	### 5.4 What this buys, and the honest limit
   486	
   487	It does **not** prove MINOR-EMPTY. What it does:
   488	
   489	* it identifies the *only* uniform theorem still standing and gives it a
   490	  precise statement: **OPEN[LADDER-NONRESONANCE-ROW]** — "for every fully
   491	  specified admissible `u_s ≥ 2` split datum with NONRES, the joint elimination
   492	  reaches a Jacobian-band row whose pivot is `(n/d_s)(n − u_s j − d_s i)` with
   493	  `1 ≤ j ≤ v_s`". The *non-vanishing* is then free (§5.2/5.3). Only the
   494	  *existence and reachability* of the row is at issue — which is exactly the
   495	  "uniform Schur/Fitting row" 17(ooooo) named, but now with its hard half
   496	  (why is the residue nonzero?) already answered on 129/177 of the stratum;
   497	* it prices the census honestly: the batch is **not** 177 undifferentiated
   498	  groups. If the ladder row transfers, 129 groups close by one theorem and 48
   499	  need a second argument — and those 48 are all at `D ≥ 144`, i.e. entirely
   500	  outside everything the campaign is currently computing;
   501	* it makes a falsifiable prediction: **`D = 108` and every `D < 144` client
   502	  must die by a Jacobian-band pivot of the stated form.** `D = 108`'s split
   503	  branch died at *stage 0 on incidence*, not on a Jacobian band (17(ddddd)) —
   504	  so either the ladder row is present but not needed there, or the mechanism is
   505	  not uniform. **Cheapest test:** re-run the `D = 108` δ=3 elimination without
   506	  the incidence block and see whether a band pivot of the predicted shape
   507	  `(n/d_s)(n − u_s j − d_s i) = 12·(108 − 2j − 9i)` appears. If it does, the
   508	  ladder is uniform across a client that dies by another route — strong
   509	  evidence. If no such row exists, OPEN[LADDER-NONRESONANCE-ROW] is refuted for
   510	  free and the census is the only (H1) deliverable. ~40 lane-minutes on the
   511	  banked `box/g108band-20260903/` driver.
   512	
   513	### 5.5 Census or theorem? — answer
   514	
   515	**Commit to the census, but run the ladder test first because it is cheap and
   516	it re-prices the census.** Concretely: the honest (H1) deliverable is the
   517	census (17(ooooo) is right), but a census of 177 groups with a corrected,
   518	timing-out engine is not obviously finishable, whereas a census of 48 groups
   519	plus one theorem is. The `D = 108` ladder test above decides which of those two
   520	you are buying, in under an hour.
