   617	## 6. The `Q*`-pivot reductions and the localisations
   618	
   619	### 6.1 The pivot rule
   620	
   621	A row is used for elimination only when some variable occurs in exactly one
   622	monomial, that monomial is the pure linear one, and its coefficient is a nonzero
   623	**rational**. Such a row is `alpha X + L = 0`, `alpha in Q*`, `L` free of `X`, and
   624	eliminating `X` is the ring isomorphism `A/(alpha X + L) ~= A'`, `X -> -L/alpha`.
   625	`rho` and `c` are forbidden as pivots, and the engine asserts after each reduction
   626	that the localisation parameter survives. So the whole elimination is a chain of
   627	ring isomorphisms and the final constant residue proves emptiness in the original
   628	presentation. (The `K2c` computation basis is unit-triangular with diagonal
   629	coefficient 1 -- the delta-5/2 gate serialised the 495-rule inverse ledger -- and
   630	a unit-triangular polynomial coordinate change preserves the unit ideal.)
